WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:11.910
Hey I'm Stuart C. Paul and this is a writer's guide to shooting a documentary. This is going to be me taking you through

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:16.830
what I learned while shooting my
own documentary the thing is when you

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.820
are making your own movie you do have to
be your own boss and that means you're

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:27.869
the one making decisions you need to
look at yourself not just as an artist

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:35.670
but as a manager of your own time you
have a certain rhythm to your life as a

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:39.710
writer and that's gonna go away it's the
same with a narrative film but

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:45.030
especially if you are a one man or a
one-woman show and you're shooting this

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.200
and you're running sound and you're
producing that means that every night

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.420
you are charging your batteries you are
offloading footage from your SD card you

00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:00.949
are formatting your cards your life will
be thrown into a certain amount of chaos

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.320
so the first point I want to make is
that your skills as a writer are your

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.040
most valuable asset it's your ability to
observe the world around you

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.290
to imagine and the tools you have with
structure your understanding of

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.400
character and conflict these are the
things that not only make a good

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.009
screenplay
they are also crucial to making a good

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:25.590
documentary I saw the story of project
unfolding in front of my eyes I go to

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:30.060
the yoga studio where one of the
documentary subjects runs her movement

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.560
therapy classes and so I saw when there
was a drunk driver that crashed into her

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.670
building
I saw them rebuilding and her struggles

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:43.380
trying to put her her world back
together and I was there the morning

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.289
that she found this piece of street art
on her building and fell in love with it

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:52.080
and heard as she struggled to get in
touch with the artist and heard the

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.899
story when they finally met and
connected and had this amazing creative

00:01:57.899 --> 00:02:03.319
collaboration that led to their idea to
make street art together and so that

00:02:03.319 --> 00:02:09.869
triggered the writer in me I saw the
scene in front of me you know of this 70

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:12.780
year old woman and this 20 year old
artist going out into the streets of Los

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:19.530
Angeles together and you know that image
that contains the story was what drove

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.489
me to say hey I think there's a story
here I think there's a documentary can I

00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:27.100
shoot it
they didn't know how they were going to

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:32.280
meld their art forms her movement in
history art to make something new and I

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.140
knew that that process was going to be a
large part of the structure and so I

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.500
basically after our first meeting went
home and I mapped out the three-act

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.850
structure I knew that we would have the
status quo of each of their worlds the

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:52.300
inciting incident the act one of them
coming together and their goal being how

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.490
do we combine our art forms to make
street art in addition I knew that ink

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.490
SEP was going to be going back to
Vietnam for the first time since he was

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:03.220
10 years old that's something that we
didn't know what was going to happen

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:09.970
there but I expected that whatever it
was would be probably a turning point

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:16.090
for the story so when he went to Vietnam
I had him shoot footage so that whatever

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:19.420
inspired him on that journey could
become part of the documentary when he

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.590
came back the end of Act two I always
expect it to be the kind of final night

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:28.660
before they go out into the streets and
the climax of the movie being them

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.410
putting up their art for the first time
together and you know seeing this

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:38.049
journey fulfilled it turned out though
that was only the middle of the movie

00:03:38.049 --> 00:03:41.769
the story kept going generally speaking
you know for writing a screenplay you

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:46.420
know where it's going to end sometimes
the characters and the story surprises

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.500
you but with a documentary you hope that
the documentary then takes on its own

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:55.780
life and the story begins going to new
places and that you are just trying to

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.989
catch up when the movie took on a life
of its own

00:03:58.989 --> 00:04:04.690
that's when things got interesting Linda
an ink SAP story it began to spread out

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:11.140
from the streets they garnered the
attention of the LA Times there is a big

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:15.640
mural that they are in talks to put up
they've even been accepted to this

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:20.430
exhibition in Budapest accepts parents
were unaware that he was a street artist

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:26.470
and so a lot of what he does in his art
is tell his family's story because they

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:31.570
are refugees from Vietnam and yet he
sort of has this separation from his

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:36.820
culture that he's working through in his
art and him coming out to his family as

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:41.890
his true authentic self
became part of the movie so there are

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:46.510
discoveries that happen along the way
and as it gets bigger you see new

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:52.919
conflicts and again that's where your
writer brain kicks in

00:04:52.919 --> 00:04:57.840
when you're writing you always want your
characters to be authentic you don't

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.509
want the hand of the writer telling them
what to do you want the truth of their

00:05:03.509 --> 00:05:09.150
voices and your actions to just flow
through you and for them to tell you

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:15.240
what happens the same thing happens with
a documentary sometimes you need to

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.909
reshoot things or you want things shot a
certain way and that's fine and it's

00:05:20.909 --> 00:05:27.090
part of the process but your key should
always be to try and capture something

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:32.639
authentic authenticity is why we watch
documentaries it's what we're looking

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:34.830
for
there's times when it seems like there's

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:39.090
nothing happening and you want to turn
off the camera and sometimes that's fine

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:44.279
if you need to you know save your SD
card if you're saving battery life but a

00:05:44.279 --> 00:05:49.439
lot of times the most authentic moments
aren't the ones that you're planning for

00:05:49.439 --> 00:05:55.680
or they just happen like when Linda and
ink SEP were driving around looking for

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.430
places to put up their street art and
they were driving past the Beverly

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:02.879
Center and Linda looks out the window
and she says there used to be a kitty

00:06:02.879 --> 00:06:05.909
park there
you could you could ride ponies there

00:06:05.909 --> 00:06:12.690
and that was just to me that was just a
beautiful authentic moment that came

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:17.250
from this woman who has been alive for
so many years who has seen the city

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:27.139
change and is relating that part of her
story to inks app into the audience

00:06:27.139 --> 00:06:31.189
one of those crucial parts of directing
a documentary is working with your

00:06:31.189 --> 00:06:38.030
documentary subjects communication is
the absolute key as our boundaries you

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:42.259
need to be clear about what those
boundaries are with your subjects what

00:06:42.259 --> 00:06:47.900
they're comfortable with what they're
not you may not know it first but you

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.189
will find out it's important to know
when to turn off the camera and when to

00:06:52.189 --> 00:06:55.520
fight to leave it on sometimes your
documentary subjects will ask you to

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.419
turn off the camera and most of the time
that's the right thing to do you should

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:06.949
respect their wishes and do so sometimes
you may want to say are you sure that's

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:10.759
necessary are you okay with me actually
continuing to film this I think it's

00:07:10.759 --> 00:07:17.629
important because sometimes their
instinct will be to avoid showing

00:07:17.629 --> 00:07:23.300
conflict on film and if it's conflict
that doesn't need to be there for the

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.689
movie that isn't important that
shouldn't be filmed and then respect

00:07:26.689 --> 00:07:32.330
that but if it is part of the story and
it's part of what is changing in their

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:36.289
world
then you should voice that and let them

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:41.560
know I think this is important I think
it's worth filming

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:47.270
I'm gonna get into a few technical tips
I picked up along the way first off if

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:51.170
you're shooting something you know know
your lenses understand photography I

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:55.430
highly recommend taking photography
class and even if there's something that

00:07:55.430 --> 00:08:00.470
doesn't seem like it would be applicable
like outdoor portraits or fashion

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:03.950
lighting you will definitely learn
something that will be useful as long as

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:09.410
you can read your histogram know your
ISO you know know your f-stops how fast

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:15.380
your lens is you'll be in good shape
hold your shot when I went back and

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:19.670
looked at the footage from the first
thing that I shot on the dock I was

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:24.230
astounded at just how often I was just
moving the camera around once you find

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:30.620
your composition stick with it go there
count to 10 in your head and let the

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:37.430
action play another thing to keep in
mind is practicality you want to be able

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:44.180
to keep your rig light and mobile and
have only what you need at the beginning

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:48.500
of the shoot like I was rigged that was
like I had my Shogun recorder I had my

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:54.740
zoom recorder I had my shotgun mic I had
my LED light and I could barely hold

00:08:54.740 --> 00:09:01.250
this thing steady it's not going to be
good footage if you're shooting 10-bit

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:05.110
but the footage is shaking all over the
place

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:11.810
strip it down to the bare essentials and
just get what you need I like to run

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:17.150
sound through an XLR adapter on top of
my camera that way I can get sound from

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:24.110
a shotgun mic and a body Michael Ave
that way I have two options for sound

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:27.980
when Linda and Inkscape were out on the
streets I would have them both miked up

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:32.780
and so I would run their labs to a zoom
recorder that I just kept recording at

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:38.900
all times and put in a pouch around my
waist and then I had my camera running

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:44.420
with a boom on top for camera sound and
then you can sync them up later in post

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:49.310
so many cameras today are so powerful
and capable of getting good footage

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:54.130
unless your shoot has a specific need to
upgrade

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:59.290
I would start with the camera that you
have

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:02.930
especially when you know if something is
coming up that is important or that

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:08.270
feels like it's out of your comfort zone
it's important to find someone to help

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:16.190
you and you may have a bigger Network
than you know for instance there were a

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:22.130
number of shoot days where something big
was going down and I knew I needed a

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:27.230
real DP to help me one was the first
time that Linda and ink SAP met to

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:31.250
collaborate on movement and sketching to
figure out the designs for the art I

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:36.230
knew I wanted to shoot it on a gimbal so
that it would be super smooth and sort

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:42.620
of reflect the fluidity of Linda's
movement in her world but I wasn't

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:47.780
comfortable using the gimbal so I called
my friend Pavel who shot The Lord of

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:51.920
Catan my last movie and he shot red
eteri last year he's an amazing

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:58.400
cinematographer and I gave him the
gimbal and knew that in his hands the

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:03.860
thing would sing and all I had to worry
about was following along and try trying

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:07.340
not to get the boom in the shot other
friends also pitched in my friend John

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:12.680
ran b cam for me a number of times and
you may feel weird reaching out to

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:17.180
people your friends and colleagues
asking them to help for little or no

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:22.880
money but if you're passionate about
your project and you can express that

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:28.240
passion to them they will want to help
you

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:33.459
think ten steps ahead to the edit when
you are shooting and you were just

00:11:33.459 --> 00:11:39.629
trying to get focus and composition and
good sound but it's important to

00:11:39.629 --> 00:11:45.279
remember to get your coverage to get
your wide shots to get your close-ups

00:11:45.279 --> 00:11:51.970
and to stay focused on whatever the core
of the scene is in the end you are going

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:58.870
to need all those pieces to make the
final film there were times where I was

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:04.600
shooting except in his studio and I was
focused on his process so a lot of the

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:09.819
shots were just of close-ups and inserts
on the art but during this he was also

00:12:09.819 --> 00:12:15.040
talking to me and so it's good that I
got his audio and I could put b-roll

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:19.569
over it but it would have been better if
I spent a little more time on his face

00:12:19.569 --> 00:12:25.209
so that I could get that personal
connection to the things he was saying

00:12:25.209 --> 00:12:29.740
you are going to be dealing with way
more footage than you have dealt with

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:35.410
before even if you shot a narrative film
and so organizing your footage is super

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:39.759
important
I had a friend run down for me his

00:12:39.759 --> 00:12:45.699
procedure and it's works pretty well for
me when you import your footage just go

00:12:45.699 --> 00:12:53.110
in and change the file names and rename
them with a prefix like a zero one for

00:12:53.110 --> 00:13:00.100
that day's import a zero two for the
next day's import B 0 1 for B cam etc if

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:07.240
you want to get really fancy add in the
date but that will give you unique file

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:11.949
names and will keep things really
organized and you will make your editor

00:13:11.949 --> 00:13:17.199
very happy later your editor
aren't you saying oh well I was gonna

00:13:17.199 --> 00:13:22.540
edit this myself I thought maybe that's
what I was going to do and then I sat

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:30.189
down and I looked at this 75 hours of
footage I had and I was like no I need

00:13:30.189 --> 00:13:34.179
someone who has done this before who has
this kind of brain who has a fresh set

00:13:34.179 --> 00:13:40.259
of eyes to help me tackle this
marketing is one of the hardest things

00:13:40.259 --> 00:13:45.600
at some point you're going to need to
raise money for us we're running a

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:52.170
Kickstarter and timing is crucially
important your network is important

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:57.959
planning is important communicating with
all the other people on your team who

00:13:57.959 --> 00:14:02.370
are going to be helping you spread the
word is big there's social media that

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:06.959
hellscape when we were planning to
launch the Kickstarter I knew that we

00:14:06.959 --> 00:14:12.749
were going to have this LA Times article
coming and for me I thought okay we will

00:14:12.749 --> 00:14:17.009
launch our Kickstarter at such a time
that the LA Times article drops in the

00:14:17.009 --> 00:14:21.360
middle of the campaign and we'll get a
big surge and that's how we're gonna

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:28.589
make our goal and then the LA Times
article dropped earlier than I expected

00:14:28.589 --> 00:14:33.420
I had set in my mind this narrative this
sequence of events and then when the LA

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:40.410
Times article dropped early that changed
and my adaptation was okay let's launch

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:44.100
faster let's use that momentum let's
just keep going

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:49.920
essentially it was the same plan whereas
perhaps what I should have done is say

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:57.899
let's step back let's reassess the new
playing field but here I am beating the

00:14:57.899 --> 00:15:01.470
drum you know doing everything I can to
get the word out to tell people about

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:06.779
this story and that's why I'm here on
film courage telling you about what I've

00:15:06.779 --> 00:15:10.520
learned
probably the biggest advice I have is

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:15.260
just to trust your gut and trust your
instincts the only times that I feel

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:21.890
like I really made a mistake where when
I didn't follow my gut feeling so as

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:29.180
writers we get in our heads we get lost
we overthink things and so if you're

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:34.850
feeling something pay attention to that
because your body is trying to tell you

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:40.130
something and when you're shooting a
documentary it's a more visceral

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:46.790
experience than just sitting and writing
and so you need to be attuned to all of

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:51.730
your senses and your instincts are a
huge part of that

00:15:51.759 --> 00:15:59.149
remember to write just a little bit will
go a long way but if that's what you're

00:15:59.149 --> 00:16:04.970
best at in the world it'll help you when
you're doing things that you're less

00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:11.179
comfortable with and it'll just keep you
grounded other than that I would say

00:16:11.179 --> 00:16:17.629
have fun you are making it happen you're
taking a risk you're picking up a camera

00:16:17.629 --> 00:16:21.709
you're going out and making a movie you
don't know what's gonna happen you don't

00:16:21.709 --> 00:16:26.629
know how it's gonna turn out and it's
exciting so that's something to be proud

00:16:26.629 --> 00:16:31.040
of because it takes you know it takes
some cojones to go do that

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:33.939
so enjoy yourself

